cartridge "SLAP SHOT/JOHN FEDOR/1989"

' SLAP SHOT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Slap Shot" by John Fedor, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, October 1989 (Commodore 64, machine language, SYS2062).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: one-on-one ice hockey seen from above. Each side has a skater
' and a goalie; the stick steers the skater and moves the goalie up and
' down at the same time. Skate over the loose puck to take it; press and
' hold the button to wind up a shot, let go to shoot (the longer the
' wind-up, the harder the shot; a full slap shot lifts the puck over the
' sticks). Without the puck, the button body-checks the other skater: he
' loses the puck and some stability, and a skater knocked to zero falls
' and must get his balance back before skating again. Blue (controller 1)
' shoots at the right goal; red (controller 2 or the computer) at the left.
' A TIME game (1-99 minutes) or a GOAL game (first to 1-99 goals); a timed
' game that ends level goes to OVERTIME: the next goal wins.
'
' From the original (orig/slapshot.prg, disassembled in orig/ss.dis, run in
' jsc64 with orig/c64run-slap.mjs and orig/c64time-slap.mjs): the rink and
' its two goals in the end boards; the title menu (TIME 05:00 or SCORE 10,
' up/down choose, left/right change, the button starts that kind of game,
' LAST GAME shown); the puck always travels on a diagonal with separate
' across/up-down speeds, bounces off the boards, and slows by one speed
' step about every 48 frames on the ice (measured: 45); skater momentum
' (the stick speeds up or slows down each axis; full speed about one C64
' pixel a frame); the skater's facing diagonal sets where he carries the
' puck and where he shoots; pressing the button with the puck winds up
' (the skater stops), the shot speed grows while the button is held and the
' shot goes when it is released or fully charged; a shot taken level with
' the goal mouth goes nearly straight, any other one at a slant; a hard
' shot lifts the puck (no skater can touch it in the air, the goalies
' still can); goalies only move up and down in front of the goal and send
' the puck back out; the body check (button, no puck, close to the other
' skater) knocks the puck loose into the air and costs stability, a fallen
' skater cannot move until his stability is back to 8, stability grows
' back by itself up to 24; overtime with "next to score a goal wins".
'
' Liberties: one player against a computer red team (the original is for
' two players only): its skater is a little slower than a human's (20/24),
' it re-plans every 10 frames and checks only half the times it could; its
' goalie only follows a puck coming its way in the last 55 pixels, at a
' quarter of the human goalie's speed, and goes back to the middle when the
' puck is far. A check costs 6 stability instead of 1 and a skater can
' check once every 40 frames; stability comes back one point every 24
' frames (on the C64 it came back so fast that nobody ever fell). Skaters
' slow down a little when the stick is let go on an axis (the C64 skater
' never stopped). The face-off puck goes toward the team that let in the
' last goal (on the C64 it always went to blue). A skater cannot take back
' his own shot for 20 frames, and when both could take the loose puck they
' take turns at being first (on the C64 red always was). The goal mouths
' and goalies are scaled so a goalie covers about the same share of the
' mouth as on the C64 (15 of 40 pixels). Overtime lasts at most 5 minutes
' (then a draw), and a two-player game with no input for 2 minutes ends, so
' every match ends. A goal shows a banner and the horn; the end names the
' winner and returns to the title. The rink is drawn at about 0.7 x 0.93
' scale.
'
' Controls: stick = skate (and the goalie up/down); button = shoot (hold
' to wind up) or body-check. Title: up/down choose a line, left/right
' change it, a button on TIME GAME or GOAL GAME starts that game; HOW TO
' PLAY and CREDITS show their pages.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off and
' the display off; music first in the sound table, the title tune once.

